= Sequence alignment =

== Overview ==
'''Sequence alignment''' is the process and resulting arrangement of two or more biological sequences so that comparable positions are placed in correspondence under a scoring or probabilistic model.

Pairwise or multiple alignments can be global, local, semi-global, structure-informed, or specialized for particular evolutionary or biological assumptions. Matches, substitutions, insertions, and deletions contribute to alignment scores.

Alignment supports similarity search, homology analysis, functional inference, structural comparison, phylogenetics, and variant analysis. An alignment is a model of correspondence, not direct proof that positions are homologous or functionally equivalent.

Different scoring matrices, gap penalties, algorithms, sequence quality, and evolutionary divergence can produce different alignments. Multiple-sequence alignment is especially model- and heuristic-dependent for large datasets.

== Boundaries ==
* alignment ≠ proof of homology
* sequence similarity ≠ homology by definition
* highest score ≠ biologically correct alignment under every model

